# November 2026: the Texas Public Radio, NPR (KGOU) and AP (KUOW) reports on Chavez and Huerta; the National Archives'
# JFK release page; the AP report (WRDW) on the Stonewall website and the NPS Stonewall homepage; Britannica, the DoD
# commemoration and James M. Lindsay (CFR) on the Tonkin House vote (the House's own pages were blocked to the fetcher);
# Senate.gov on the 1964 cloture vote; the NARA pages on EO 10730 and the Tonkin Gulf Resolution; the House History page
# on the Southern Manifesto; the NPS Till monument release; the Letter from Birmingham Jail (Penn); Amos 5:24 (NRSVUE);
# the Kerner Report extract (History Matters); the NBER abstract of Burkhauser et al.; the Manhattan Institute page on
# Losing Ground; the H-Diplo roundtable on Triumph Forsaken; Kort's introduction (Cambridge); the Office of the Historian
# on the end of the war; the White House RFK Files page and PBS SoCal.
# Chart figures are copied from the DOJ registration table, the Census poverty series (FRED) and Moise's troop table with
# the National Archives' deaths by year.
# Scholars quoted only from their own work (abstracts, roundtable texts, Kort's introduction). Murray is given as the
# Manhattan Institute describes his book, and Bailey and Danziger as their publisher describes theirs, without quotation
# marks. Chapman and Gardner are quoted as Moyar quotes them in the same roundtable.
# Left out: the Senate's final 1964 vote and any party breakdown of it (not opened; only the cloture coalition is given);
# Money, Mississippi (no opened source places events there); a day count for the bus boycott; the exact Anniston date;
# Britannica's dates for the 1968 convention (no URL in the research); the phrase 'police riot'; the 1979 House committee;
# Herring's and Sorley's own arguments (not opened; Kort ties no thesis to Herring); Huerta's statement about children
# (not given verbatim in the opened report); the Freedom Summer volunteer count; the Gulf of Tonkin, Hue, Kent and Jackson
# points (P8 is a chart).
